1
00:00:00,093 --> 00:00:03,297
A language model reads a text with one thousand words.

2
00:00:03,776 --> 00:00:08,736
For each word, it gives the correct word a probability, say one in a hundred.

3
00:00:09,148 --> 00:00:14,148
To score the whole text, we multiply all one thousand probabilities.

4
00:00:14,561 --> 00:00:17,526
Ask a computer to do that, and it answers zero.

5
00:00:17,952 --> 00:00:18,856
Not a small number.

6
00:00:19,268 --> 00:00:20,292
Exactly zero.

7
00:00:20,731 --> 00:00:24,055
The model has learned something, but the computer cannot see it.

8
00:00:25,006 --> 00:00:29,620
Two functions fix this problem: the exponential and the logarithm.

9
00:00:30,046 --> 00:00:30,697
They are a pair.

10
00:00:31,136 --> 00:00:32,652
One undoes the other.

11
00:00:33,078 --> 00:00:39,181
In this video you will learn what they mean, their key rules, and why machine learning uses logs everywhere.

12
00:00:39,580 --> 00:00:41,655
You need school arithmetic with powers.

13
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:45,152
For the slopes, you need the derivative from video seven.

14
00:00:46,859 --> 00:00:48,375
Start with repeated growth.

15
00:00:48,774 --> 00:00:51,567
A bacterium splits in two every hour.

16
00:00:52,006 --> 00:00:53,561
After one hour you have two.

17
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,463
After two hours, four.

18
00:00:55,902 --> 00:00:57,404
After three hours, eight.

19
00:00:57,830 --> 00:01:02,112
That is two times two times two, written two to the power three.

20
00:01:02,551 --> 00:01:05,410
The power counts how many times we multiply.

21
00:01:06,939 --> 00:01:08,189
Now a special base.

22
00:01:08,615 --> 00:01:11,687
Suppose money grows by one hundred percent per year.

23
00:01:12,139 --> 00:01:14,559
Paid once, one euro becomes two.

24
00:01:14,984 --> 00:01:20,476
Paid twelve times, in small monthly pieces, it becomes about two point six one.

25
00:01:20,902 --> 00:01:23,894
Paid every day, about two point seven one.

26
00:01:24,333 --> 00:01:29,200
Paid all the time, it approaches a fixed number: e, about two point seven one eight.

27
00:01:30,739 --> 00:01:34,237
The function e to the power x is called the exponential.

28
00:01:34,649 --> 00:01:35,526
Look at its graph.

29
00:01:35,952 --> 00:01:38,385
At x equals zero, it is one.

30
00:01:38,798 --> 00:01:41,165
At x equals one, it is e.

31
00:01:41,604 --> 00:01:44,675
At x equals two, about seven point three nine.

32
00:01:45,088 --> 00:01:49,649
To the left, it gets smaller and smaller, but it never reaches zero.

33
00:01:50,088 --> 00:01:52,574
The exponential is always positive.

34
00:01:54,106 --> 00:01:56,420
The exponential has one key rule.

35
00:01:56,819 --> 00:02:00,781
e to the a plus b equals e to the a times e to the b.

36
00:02:01,207 --> 00:02:01,526
Why?

37
00:02:01,952 --> 00:02:04,718
e squared is two copies of e multiplied.

38
00:02:05,130 --> 00:02:07,098
e cubed is three copies.

39
00:02:07,537 --> 00:02:09,412
Together, that is five copies.

40
00:02:09,851 --> 00:02:19,119
Check it: seven point three eight nine times twenty point zero eight six is one hundred forty eight point four one, which is e to the five.

41
00:02:20,826 --> 00:02:22,076
Now turn the question around.

42
00:02:22,448 --> 00:02:25,959
e to what power gives seven point three eight nine?

43
00:02:26,384 --> 00:02:27,249
The answer is two.

44
00:02:27,688 --> 00:02:31,797
This question, what power gives this number, is called the logarithm.

45
00:02:32,236 --> 00:02:35,201
We write the natural logarithm as l n.

46
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:39,935
So l n of seven point three eight nine is two.

47
00:02:41,473 --> 00:02:44,159
The logarithm undoes the exponential.

48
00:02:44,584 --> 00:02:49,837
Put x into the exponential machine, then put the result into the logarithm machine.

49
00:02:50,262 --> 00:02:51,007
You get x back.

50
00:02:51,446 --> 00:02:54,770
So l n of e to the x equals x.

51
00:02:55,183 --> 00:03:02,749
On the graph, the log curve is the mirror image of the exponential curve, reflected across the line y equals x.

52
00:03:03,673 --> 00:03:04,657
Read some values.

53
00:03:05,069 --> 00:03:09,484
l n of one is zero, because e to the zero is one.

54
00:03:09,923 --> 00:03:11,811
l n of e is one.

55
00:03:12,276 --> 00:03:16,518
For numbers between zero and one, the logarithm is negative.

56
00:03:16,931 --> 00:03:21,691
And the logarithm of zero or of a negative number does not exist.

57
00:03:22,130 --> 00:03:27,782
No power of e can give zero, because e to the x is always positive.

58
00:03:29,532 --> 00:03:32,179
Here is the most useful rule of logs.

59
00:03:32,604 --> 00:03:36,115
The log of a times b equals the log of a plus the log of b.

60
00:03:36,607 --> 00:03:38,070
Products become sums.

61
00:03:38,495 --> 00:03:39,227
Why is it true?

62
00:03:39,652 --> 00:03:43,868
Write a as e to the p, and b as e to the q.

63
00:03:44,293 --> 00:03:47,471
Then a times b is e to the p plus q.

64
00:03:47,897 --> 00:03:50,344
Its logarithm is p plus q.

65
00:03:51,859 --> 00:03:52,936
Let's check it with numbers.

66
00:03:53,375 --> 00:03:56,673
l n of two is about zero point six nine three.

67
00:03:57,112 --> 00:04:00,476
l n of three is about one point zero nine nine.

68
00:04:00,902 --> 00:04:03,309
Add them: one point seven nine two.

69
00:04:03,734 --> 00:04:09,652
And l n of six, computed directly, is also one point seven nine two.

70
00:04:10,077 --> 00:04:11,035
The rule works.

71
00:04:12,573 --> 00:04:14,075
Two more rules follow from it.

72
00:04:14,514 --> 00:04:22,971
The log of a power: the log of a to the n equals n times the log of a, because a power is repeated multiplication.

73
00:04:23,384 --> 00:04:27,506
And the log of a division is the log of the top minus the log of the bottom.

74
00:04:27,945 --> 00:04:31,309
Multiply becomes add, divide becomes subtract.

75
00:04:32,839 --> 00:04:37,826
Before computers, people used printed tables of logarithms for exactly this reason.

76
00:04:38,265 --> 00:04:45,140
To multiply two long numbers, they looked up the two logs, added them, and looked up the answer.

77
00:04:45,565 --> 00:04:47,999
Adding is much easier than multiplying.

78
00:04:48,438 --> 00:04:52,454
Machine learning uses the same trick today, for a different reason.

79
00:04:54,173 --> 00:04:57,178
A probability is a number between zero and one.

80
00:04:57,603 --> 00:05:00,556
Its logarithm is zero or negative.

81
00:05:00,994 --> 00:05:03,149
Probability one gives zero.

82
00:05:03,601 --> 00:05:06,513
One half gives about minus zero point six nine.

83
00:05:06,952 --> 00:05:10,050
One in a hundred gives minus four point six one.

84
00:05:10,489 --> 00:05:15,635
And zero point zero zero zero one gives minus nine point two one.

85
00:05:16,061 --> 00:05:18,933
Tiny numbers become friendly negative numbers.

86
00:05:19,873 --> 00:05:20,670
Notice the pattern.

87
00:05:21,083 --> 00:05:28,676
Every time the probability gets ten times smaller, the log goes down by the same step, about two point three.

88
00:05:29,115 --> 00:05:32,266
The log measures how many factors of ten we lost.

89
00:05:32,678 --> 00:05:38,583
It is like a ruler that counts zeros, so very small numbers stay easy to read and compare.

90
00:05:40,126 --> 00:05:41,243
Now the computer problem.

91
00:05:41,668 --> 00:05:50,246
A standard computer number, called float sixty four, cannot store positive numbers smaller than about ten to the minus three hundred twenty four.

92
00:05:50,658 --> 00:05:53,038
Anything smaller is rounded to zero.

93
00:05:53,464 --> 00:05:54,661
This is called underflow.

94
00:05:55,086 --> 00:06:02,746
Multiply probabilities of one in a hundred, and the product hits zero after only one hundred sixty two factors.

95
00:06:04,286 --> 00:06:06,254
With logs, there is no problem.

96
00:06:06,679 --> 00:06:09,273
The log of the product is the sum of the logs.

97
00:06:09,711 --> 00:06:15,735
One thousand times minus four point six one is about minus four thousand six hundred five.

98
00:06:16,174 --> 00:06:17,185
That is a normal number.

99
00:06:17,624 --> 00:06:22,969
Also, the log keeps the order: a bigger probability always has a bigger log.

100
00:06:23,408 --> 00:06:26,041
So the best answer stays the best answer.

101
00:06:27,739 --> 00:06:28,643
Now the slopes.

102
00:06:29,056 --> 00:06:33,218
Remember the derivative from video seven: the slope of a curve at a point.

103
00:06:33,657 --> 00:06:36,157
The exponential has a beautiful property.

104
00:06:36,582 --> 00:06:38,457
Its derivative is itself.

105
00:06:38,896 --> 00:06:42,327
The slope of e to the x equals e to the x.

106
00:06:42,606 --> 00:06:43,763
Watch the tangent line.

107
00:06:44,202 --> 00:06:47,021
At zero, height one and slope one.

108
00:06:47,447 --> 00:06:50,385
At one, height and slope are both e.

109
00:06:51,906 --> 00:06:55,496
The derivative of the logarithm is one over x.

110
00:06:55,909 --> 00:06:59,100
At x equals one, the slope is one.

111
00:06:59,539 --> 00:07:02,903
At x equals four, the slope is one quarter.

112
00:07:03,329 --> 00:07:05,948
Near zero, the slope is huge.

113
00:07:06,387 --> 00:07:09,073
At zero point zero one, it is one hundred.

114
00:07:09,512 --> 00:07:16,653
In learning, this means a tiny probability on the right answer gives a big slope, so the model is pushed hard.

115
00:07:18,173 --> 00:07:20,965
Where do exp and log come from in our series?

116
00:07:21,391 --> 00:07:23,266
They are not one of the five axioms.

117
00:07:23,691 --> 00:07:27,042
They are school math, the floor that the axioms stand on.

118
00:07:27,494 --> 00:07:35,566
But together with the chain rule from video nine, these two slopes are enough to differentiate most loss functions in machine learning.

119
00:07:37,372 --> 00:07:39,779
First use: making numbers positive.

120
00:07:40,205 --> 00:07:44,021
A classifier produces scores, and scores can be negative.

121
00:07:44,434 --> 00:07:46,481
But probabilities must be positive.

122
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:49,806
So we apply the exponential to every score.

123
00:07:50,245 --> 00:07:58,609
Scores two, one, and minus one become seven point three nine, two point seven two, and zero point three seven.

124
00:07:59,035 --> 00:07:59,753
All positive.

125
00:08:00,673 --> 00:08:03,651
Then divide by the total, about ten point four eight.

126
00:08:04,064 --> 00:08:11,204
We get zero point seven zero five, zero point two five nine, and zero point zero three five.

127
00:08:11,643 --> 00:08:12,375
They add up to one.

128
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:15,154
This recipe is called softmax.

129
00:08:15,579 --> 00:08:20,407
It is a molecule built from this atom, and we will meet it again later in the series.

130
00:08:21,939 --> 00:08:24,067
Second use: the log-likelihood.

131
00:08:24,492 --> 00:08:29,240
To score a whole data set, we multiply the probabilities of all examples.

132
00:08:29,679 --> 00:08:36,128
Take three examples with probabilities zero point nine, zero point eight, and zero point five.

133
00:08:36,580 --> 00:08:39,133
The product is zero point three six.

134
00:08:39,572 --> 00:08:47,285
The sum of the logs is minus one point zero two two, which is exactly the log of zero point three six.

135
00:08:48,806 --> 00:08:53,114
So training can maximize a sum of logs instead of a fragile product.

136
00:08:53,540 --> 00:08:58,700
Deep learning code prefers to minimize, so it flips the sign and takes the average.

137
00:08:59,125 --> 00:09:03,194
That is the cross-entropy loss, which we meet in video fifteen.

138
00:09:03,620 --> 00:09:13,859
And libraries go one step further for numerical stability: they compute the log directly from the scores, without ever forming the tiny probability.

139
00:09:15,606 --> 00:09:16,151
Your turn.

140
00:09:16,630 --> 00:09:18,505
Pause the video and try this.

141
00:09:18,944 --> 00:09:26,789
You know that l n of two is about zero point six nine three and l n of five is about one point six zero nine.

142
00:09:27,242 --> 00:09:30,313
Without a calculator, what is l n of ten?

143
00:09:31,239 --> 00:09:33,287
Ten is two times five.

144
00:09:33,686 --> 00:09:35,880
A log of a product is a sum of logs.

145
00:09:36,319 --> 00:09:44,643
So l n of ten is zero point six nine three plus one point six zero nine, which is about two point three zero.

146
00:09:45,082 --> 00:09:48,473
That is also the step we saw for every factor of ten.

147
00:09:50,006 --> 00:09:50,817
Second question.

148
00:09:51,216 --> 00:09:55,511
A model gives four examples the probability one half each.

149
00:09:55,937 --> 00:09:58,397
What is the product of the four probabilities?

150
00:09:58,836 --> 00:10:00,312
And what is the sum of their logs?

151
00:10:00,737 --> 00:10:05,578
Use l n of one half, about minus zero point six nine three.

152
00:10:06,003 --> 00:10:06,668
Pause and try.

153
00:10:07,606 --> 00:10:14,734
The product is one half to the power four, which is one sixteenth, or zero point zero six two five.

154
00:10:15,186 --> 00:10:21,662
The sum of logs is four times minus zero point six nine three, about minus two point seven seven.

155
00:10:22,087 --> 00:10:28,603
And l n of zero point zero six two five is indeed about minus two point seven seven.

156
00:10:30,306 --> 00:10:32,434
Let's check it in Python with NumPy.

157
00:10:32,872 --> 00:10:35,771
We test the exponential rule and the log rule.

158
00:10:36,197 --> 00:10:40,279
Then we make one thousand probabilities of zero point zero one.

159
00:10:40,718 --> 00:10:46,011
We multiply them with n p dot prod, and we add their logs with n p dot sum.

160
00:10:46,450 --> 00:10:49,309
We also find when the product first becomes zero.

161
00:10:50,839 --> 00:10:54,709
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

162
00:10:55,121 --> 00:10:58,486
e squared times e cubed equals e to the five.

163
00:10:58,884 --> 00:11:01,930
l n two plus l n three equals l n six.

164
00:11:02,355 --> 00:11:07,914
The product of one thousand probabilities is zero point zero, exactly zero.

165
00:11:08,339 --> 00:11:11,850
It became zero after one hundred sixty two factors.

166
00:11:12,289 --> 00:11:17,541
But the sum of the logs is minus four thousand six hundred five point one seven.

167
00:11:17,967 --> 00:11:19,709
The information is safe.

168
00:11:21,439 --> 00:11:22,091
In summary.

169
00:11:22,530 --> 00:11:29,591
The exponential, e to the x, is repeated growth with base e, about two point seven one eight.

170
00:11:30,030 --> 00:11:33,594
It is always positive, and it turns sums into products.

171
00:11:34,019 --> 00:11:38,128
The logarithm answers the question: what power gives this number?

172
00:11:38,540 --> 00:11:42,756
It undoes the exponential, and it turns products into sums.

173
00:11:43,673 --> 00:11:49,603
Logs turn tiny probabilities into friendly negative numbers and prevent underflow.

174
00:11:50,042 --> 00:11:57,263
The slope of e to the x is e to the x, and the slope of l n x is one over x.

175
00:11:57,689 --> 00:12:04,896
Next, in video eleven, we start probability: a random variable, a way to turn chance into numbers.

176
00:12:06,839 --> 00:12:08,661
This video was made by David R.

177
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:12,331
You can find more lessons at David Ramirez J dot com.

178
00:12:12,890 --> 00:12:13,608
Thanks for watching.
